129.m:
Indra said:
My enemy was powerful, and like Saturn to behold;
by him I was bound, brought down low, and repeatedly humiliated.

Even while residing in heaven, Indra constantly follows that Vṛṣākapi.
Seeing him attached to another, Śacī became angry with love;
comforting her, Śatamanyu said this with a laugh:

135.m:
Brahmā said:
The confluence of Vāṇī is renowned, where Hara is the Lord of Speech;
that tīrtha frees from all sins and grants all desires.

139.m:
Having cut down this doubt, a creature attains the highest desired object.
Desire enters like a female goblin and burns away all happiness;
cutting it down with the sword of perfect 'I'-ness, one attains liberation while living.

140.m:
There he saw the thousand-eyed Indra, surrounded by auspicious gods,
praised by the Siddhas and Sādhyas, watching excellent dancing,
listening to sweet song, and fanned by Apsarases;

144.m:
The Fire said:
In the beginning, [she is] a wife (patnī); from being maintained (bharaṇa), she is a bhāryā;
from giving birth (janana), she is a jāyā; by her own virtues, she is a spouse (kalatra).
O blessed one, you bear forms such as these;
carry out the word that has been spoken by me.

146.m:
Brahmā said:
There is another holy place, the Yāyāta, where Śiva is Kālañjara;
it quiets all sins, and its history is told by me.
